  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\n(Tuesday Night - Friday Night)\n\nThe stagnant weather pattern across the CONUS should continue \nthrough at least the end of the work week, with very anomalous (500-\nhPa heights >99th percentile of climatology) mid/upper level ridging \nacross the mid-Mississippi Valley. Temperatures at 850 hPa also \nremain > +20C, or above the 95th percentile of climatology. We \nshould see a continuation of high temperatures in the mid to upper \n90s each day, with lows in the 70s to near 80 degrees. There remains \nsome skepticism at least of the dewpoint increase forecast by the \nNBM. With little/no change in the ambient air mass and any frontal \nboundary north of the CWA (where dewpoints may pool), it is \ndifficult to see much of an increase, if any at all. \nEvapotranspiration rates are quite a bit less than 4-6 weeks ago. In \naddition, if anything, the soils will continue to dry out with each \nsuccessive hot and dry day that transpires. Some increase may be \nwarranted due to a bit better moisture return from the Gulf, but it \nis taking the long path across the northern Gulf into Texas and then \nnorth/northeast through the south-central Plains into the mid-\nMississippi Valley. However, southwest surface winds are also \ndownsloping off of the Ozark Plateau so that may counteract the \nslightly higher dewpoints at least attempting to advect into the \nregion. Long story short, peak heat index values may tend to \nincrease slightly, with more widespread 100-105 degree values toward \nthe end of the week. Thursday and Friday afternoon are more likely \nto see more pockets of 105+ values as well. Even if these higher \nheat index values verify, they would be quite a bit less than \nearlier heat waves this summer, due to lower humidity. In addition, \nthe lower sun angle of early September is a far cry from June/July \n(similar to early/mid April), days are shorter, and there has been a \nwhole summer of acclimation to the heat.\n\n\n(Saturday - Labor Day)\n\nForecast uncertainty does increase a bit for the Labor Day holiday \nweekend. There remains a signal for the mid/upper level ridge to \nshift slightly westward more into the Plains, with a trough digging \ninto the northeast. This is a favorable pattern for a backdoor cold \nfront to pass through the Great Lakes and much of the Ohio Valley, \nbut there is less confidence in how far southwest the boundary will \nreach. Backdoor fronts are often difficult to pin down this far out, \nand this is no exception. How far that front is able to make it into \nour area, if at all, likely will come down to how far west the ridge \naxis retrogrades, as well as how strong the trough is coming into \nthe northeast. Two of the four clusters from the LREF of the 500-hPa \nheight pattern would be favorable for the boundary to make it to \nnear the Mississippi River, while the other two barely make it into \nsouth-central Illinois. This means that the late summer heat wave \nprobably will last into, if not through (and possibly beyond), the \nholiday weekend for central/southeast Missouri. There is less \nconfidence the farther northeast you head, but at least some cooling \nseems likely especially as you get into west central and south-\ncentral Illinois. An increase in cloud cover and afternoon/early \nevening thunderstorm chances (20-30%) could also help take the edge \noff of the heat. While the specific temperatures are not certain, \neven the potential for a backdoor cold front would probably only get \nreadings back within 5-8 degrees of normal. Dewpoints also likely \nwill increase near this front, so heat index values may stay near \n100 degrees. It is looking quite likely that this first full week of \nSeptember will be one of the warmest, if not the warmest, at our \nofficial climate stations (KSTL, KCOU, KUIN). Taking a peek toward \nweek 2, the anomalously warm pattern is likely to continue, and if \nso, could mark the warmest first half of September on record. In \nother words, at this point in time there is no DEFINITIVE end in \nsight for this ongoing heat wave.",
